.TH login "" "Command"
.SH NAME
login \- log in or change user name
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlogin [\-q] [\fIusername\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
login -- log in or change user name
Usage:	login [ -q ] [ username ]
Option:	-q	Do not print the news
.HE
.B login
identifies a user to the system.
It is normally invoked automatically, on a terminal line, by
.BR getty ;
a user may also invoke it directly (typically with
.B "exec /bin/login"
from a shell) to change to another login name without disconnecting
the line.
.PP
If
.I username
is not given on the command line,
.B login
prompts
.BR "Coherent login:" .
It then looks up the name in
.BR /etc/passwd .
If the account has a password,
.B login
prompts for it with echoing turned off, encrypts what was typed with
.BR crypt() ,
and compares the result against the encrypted password on file.
If the terminal line is a remote (dial-in) line,
.B login
also asks for a second, remote-access password, checked against a
pseudo-account named
.BR remacc .
.PP
After three failed remote attempts, or if the user does not finish
logging in within the timeout period,
.B login
gives up and, for a remote line, tries to force a hangup.
Every attempt, successful or not, is logged to
.B /etc/utmp
and either
.B /usr/adm/wtmp
or
.BR /usr/adm/failed .
.PP
Once the password check succeeds,
.B login
changes to the user's home directory, sets the process's user and
group IDs, and, unless
.B \-q
was given, prints the contents of
.B /etc/motd
if it has changed since this user last logged in, and any new
material in
.BR /etc/news ,
together with a
.B "You have mail."
notice if the user's mailbox is not empty.
It then sets up a minimal environment
(\fIPATH\fR, \fIHOME\fR, and \fIPS1\fR)
and executes the user's shell, as recorded in the last field of the
.B /etc/passwd
entry (or
.B /bin/sh
if none is recorded).
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 24n
\fB/etc/passwd\fR	password file
\fB/etc/motd\fR	message of the day
\fB/etc/news\fR	news file
\fB/etc/newslog\fR	per-user record of news already read
\fB/etc/utmp\fR	identifiers of users currently logged in
\fB/usr/adm/wtmp\fR	history of successful logins
\fB/usr/adm/failed\fR	history of failed login attempts
\fB/usr/spool/mail/\fIname\fR	mailbox for \fIuser\fR
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR passwd ,
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B login
prints
.B "Login incorrect"
and re-prompts if the name or password does not match, and
.B "No shell."
if it cannot execute the user's shell.
.SH NOTES
Unless it was invoked with
.B argv[0][0]
equal to
.B \-
(as done by
.BR getty ),
.B login
resets the terminal's tty modes and special characters to the
system's compiled-in defaults before prompting.
